::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does MLA.GEO.FND.1 require students to construct? {
~A rough sketch of any shape#A rough sketch does not meet the precision required for copying a segment or angle.
~A written definition only#Definitions support communication, but the standard requires making a construction.
=A copy of a segment or an angle using geometric tools#Correct. The standard focuses on copying a segment or an angle with geometric tools.
~A drawing based only on appearance#Appearance alone does not provide construction evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is most important for transferring a segment length exactly? {
=Compass#Correct. A compass holds a fixed opening and transfers a segment length.
~Calculator#A calculator does not transfer a physical segment length in a construction.
~Protractor#A protractor measures angles, not segment lengths.
~Notebook margin#A margin is not a geometric construction tool.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is used to draw the starting ray for a copied angle? {
~Compass only#A compass draws arcs, but a straightedge is used for a straight ray.
=Straightedge#Correct. A straightedge is used to draw the starting ray for a copied angle.
~Calculator#A calculator cannot draw a geometric ray.
~Ruler marks only#Measurement marks are not needed to draw a straight ray in this construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>To copy segment AB starting at point C, what is the first drawing step? {
~Draw a shaded region around C.#Shading does not create the path needed for the copied segment.
~Measure angle ABC.#The task is copying a segment, not measuring an angle.
~Erase point C.#Point C is the starting point for the copied segment.
=Draw a ray with endpoint C.#Correct. The ray provides the path on which the copied segment endpoint can be marked.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>When copying segment AB, why is the compass opened from A to B? {
~To measure an angle at A#The compass opening transfers distance, not angle measure.
~To make the drawing darker#The purpose is to capture a distance, not change the appearance of the drawing.
=To capture the length of segment AB#Correct. The compass opening stores the distance from A to B.
~To rename segment AB#Opening the compass transfers distance; it does not rename the segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does keeping the compass opening fixed accomplish when copying a segment? {
~It changes the copied length.#Changing length is exactly what fixed compass settings prevent.
=It preserves the original length.#Correct. A fixed compass opening transfers the same distance to the new ray.
~It measures the angle.#A fixed compass opening transfers distance, not angle measure by itself.
~It changes the segment name.#A compass setting transfers distance; it does not change labels.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>If segment RS is constructed as a copy of segment PQ, which statement should be true? {
=RS = PQ#Correct. A copied segment is congruent to the original segment.
~RS is longer than PQ#A copy should match the original length.
~RS is shorter than PQ#A copy should not be shorter than the original segment.
~RS has no relationship to PQ#A copied segment should match the original segment length.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which action would make a copied segment inaccurate? {
~Drawing a ray from the new starting point#A starting ray is part of the segment-copy construction.
~Placing the compass point on the new starting point#This is needed to transfer the segment length.
=Changing the compass opening before marking the endpoint#Correct. Changing the opening changes the transferred distance.
~Marking the endpoint where the arc meets the ray#This creates the copied segment endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Why is estimating by sight not enough when copying a segment? {
~A copied segment should be longer than the original.#A copy should match the original, not be longer.
~Sight estimation always gives exact results.#Sight estimation does not guarantee exact copied length.
~It requires too many arcs.#The issue is accuracy, not the number of arcs.
=It does not guarantee the copied length equals the original length.#Correct. A construction needs tool evidence that preserves the distance.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>To copy &angle;ABC, where is the first arc of the original angle centered? {
=At B#Correct. The first arc is centered at the vertex of the original angle.
~At A#The original angle's vertex is B, not A.
~At C#C is on one side of the angle, not the vertex.
~At a point chosen by sight#The arc must be centered at the original angle vertex, not an estimated point.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>When copying an angle, why is a matching arc drawn from the new vertex? {
~To decorate the new drawing#The matching arc is construction evidence, not decoration.
~To measure the original segment length#The arc helps transfer the angle opening, not a segment length alone.
~To erase the original angle#The original angle remains as the source for the copy.
=To create a corresponding arc for the copied angle#Correct. The matching arc gives a place to transfer the original angle opening.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>What distance is transferred after drawing the arc across both sides of the original angle? {
~The distance from the vertex to the page edge#The page edge is not part of the angle-copy construction.
=The distance between the two arc intersections on the original angle#Correct. This distance determines where the second ray of the copied angle should pass.
~The length of the longest ray#Rays do not have a fixed length.
~The distance between unrelated points#Unrelated distances do not preserve the original angle opening.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;DEF is constructed as a copy of &angle;ABC, which statement should be true? {
~m&angle;DEF > m&angle;ABC#A copy should not be larger than the original.
~m&angle;DEF < m&angle;ABC#A copy should not be smaller than the original.
=m&angle;DEF = m&angle;ABC#Correct. A copied angle should have the same measure as the original angle.
~DE = AB#Copying an angle preserves angle measure, not necessarily these segment lengths.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which action would make an angle copy unreliable? {
=Placing the second ray by sight#Correct. The second ray should be placed using arc intersections and compass distances.
~Drawing an initial ray from the new vertex#An initial ray is needed for the copied angle.
~Drawing an arc centered at the original vertex#This is part of the angle-copy construction.
~Using the compass to transfer an arc distance#This is part of a valid angle-copy construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Why should a copied angle be checked with construction evidence instead of appearance? {
~A copied angle should look larger than the original.#A copy should match the original angle measure.
=Appearance alone does not prove the angle measure was preserved.#Correct. Arcs and compass distances provide construction evidence.
~Angle copies do not require tools.#MLA.GEO.FND.1 requires geometric tools.
~A copied angle is always a right angle.#Copying an angle preserves the original angle measure, which may not be 90&deg;.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes the role of a compass in copying an angle? {
~It measures the angle in degrees.#A compass does not measure degrees.
~It labels the new angle automatically.#Labels must be added by the student.
=It draws arcs and transfers distances between arc intersections.#Correct. These actions preserve the angle opening.
~It chooses labels for the angle.#Labels name parts of a figure; the compass transfers distances.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly describes the role of a straightedge in these constructions? {
~It transfers distances by holding a fixed opening.#That is the role of a compass.
~It measures angle size in degrees.#A protractor measures degrees, not a straightedge.
~It chooses the correct answer automatically.#A tool does not replace reasoning or construction steps.
=It draws straight rays, lines, or segments.#Correct. A straightedge is used for straight parts of the construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student copies segment AB and writes CD = AB. What does this statement mean? {
~CD is an angle copy of AB.#CD and AB name segments, not angles.
=The copied segment CD has the same length as segment AB.#Correct. CD = AB states equal segment lengths.
~CD is a different length from AB.#The statement CD = AB means equal lengths, not different lengths.
~CD is longer than AB.#The equality statement means the lengths match.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student copies &angle;ABC and writes m&angle;DEF = m&angle;ABC. What does this statement mean? {
=The copied angle DEF has the same measure as angle ABC.#Correct. The statement shows the angle measure was preserved.
~The sides of the angles have equal segment lengths.#Equal angle measure does not necessarily mean the side lengths match.
~The copied angle is larger than the original.#The equality statement means the measures match.
~The copied angle is a segment.#An angle is not a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which construction belongs in MLA.GEO.FND.1? {
~Estimating the length of AB by sight#Estimation does not provide precise construction evidence.
~Writing only the definition of a segment#Definitions help, but the standard requires construction.
~Labeling AB without drawing a copy#Labels alone do not construct a copied segment.
=Copying segment AB onto a new ray#Correct. Copying a segment is part of MLA.GEO.FND.1.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which construction belongs in MLA.GEO.FND.1? {
~Estimating the angle opening by sight#Estimation does not provide precise construction evidence.
~Writing only the definition of an angle#Definitions help, but the standard requires construction.
=Copying &angle;ABC from a new vertex#Correct. Copying an angle is part of MLA.GEO.FND.1.
~Labeling the angle without drawing a copy#Labels alone do not construct a copied angle.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student changes the compass opening after measuring segment AB and before marking point D. What is the error? {
=The copied length may no longer equal AB.#Correct. Changing the compass opening changes the transferred distance.
~The student used a ray.#Using a ray as the path for the copied segment is appropriate.
~The student copied the angle correctly.#The question is about copying a segment, not an angle.
~The labels automatically become correct.#Labels do not fix a changed compass opening.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>A student copies an angle but skips transferring the distance between the original arc intersections. What is the likely problem? {
~The segment length is still guaranteed.#The question is about copying an angle, not a segment length.
=The second ray may not preserve the original angle measure.#Correct. That transferred distance helps place the second ray accurately.
~The copied angle must be exactly 90&deg;.#Skipping a construction step does not guarantee a right angle.
~The angle name automatically changes.#Renaming does not fix a missing construction step.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation best supports a correct segment copy? {
~The copy looks close to the original.#Appearance alone is not construction evidence.
~The copied segment is drawn in the same color.#Color does not prove equal length.
~The copied segment is on the same page.#Location on the page does not prove equal length.
=The compass opening from the original segment was transferred without changing it.#Correct. This explains how the copied segment preserves the original length.
}

::GEO_U01_L04_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation best supports a correct angle copy? {
=Matching arcs and transferred compass distances were used to place the second ray.#Correct. This explains how the original angle opening was preserved.
~The copied angle looks about the same size.#Appearance alone is not construction evidence.
~The copied angle was drawn near the original.#Location does not prove equal angle measure.
~The copied angle was labeled with different letters.#Labels identify the angle but do not prove the measure was preserved.
}

